          %h3#variant_proofs Variant proofs
          %p
            There are actually quite a few proofs of Proposition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#if"}
            that appear in the literature. The proof given
            %a{:href => "#ifProof"} above
            might have been the result of a gradual whittling down from more abstract, high-powered-looking proofs.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                One “canonical” proof that a seasoned mathematician might dream up goes as follows. Consider
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo =
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = \beta({|X|})
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilters"} ultrafilters
                on the underlying set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|}
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone-Cech+compactification"} Stone-Cech compactification
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                (retopologized as a discrete space). Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \subseteq Y \times X
                be the convergence relation (i.e.,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U, x) \in K
                iff the ultrafilter
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                on the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                converges to the point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                in the space
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                is closed. Thus
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(K) \subseteq Y
                is closed by hypothesis. Also observe that the “diagonal”
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi prin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta = \{(prin(x), x): x \in X\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi prin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prin(x)
                is the principal ultrafilter generated by
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi prin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prin(x)
                converges to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(K)
                also contains all principal ultrafilters which form a dense subset of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(K)
                is all of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                there is some
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U, x) \in K
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                On reflection, we didn’t really have to consider the entire space
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta({|X|})
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                converges, we can just look at the subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|} \cup \{U\} \hookrightarrow \beta({|X|})
                with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
                coming from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta({|X|})
                and run through the same diagonal proof. This gives us the proof found in Bourbaki’s General Topology: given an ultrafilter
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                on the underlying set of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                whose underlying set is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|} \sqcup \{\infty\}
                and topologized by saying all subsets of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                are open, and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \sqcup \{\infty\}
                is an open neighborhood of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                if
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in U
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta({|X|})
            %li
              %p
                On further reflection, the ultrafilter convergence characterization of compact spaces does rely on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+theorem"} ultrafilter theorem
                (that every proper filter can be extended to an ultrafilter), which involves the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                or some choice-y principle outside ZF. If one doesn’t like this, one can always use just
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
                instead, and use the characterization of compact spaces that says that every proper filter on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|}
                admits a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cluster+point"} cluster point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo =
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = {|X|} \sqcup \{\infty\}
                but use a given proper filter
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                to describe the neighborhoods of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                looks just the same as before.
            %li
              %p
                On still further reflection, one doesn’t have to mention filters or cluster points at all. Instead one can work directly with a collection of sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_i
                satisfying the finite intersection property, and secretly think of that as generating a filter (a proper one by the FIP) and then follow 3. to define the topology of the space
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_i
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#if"}
            %li#StillOtherCleverConstructions
              %p
                Still other clever constructions of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                are available. One has been described by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mart%C3%ADn+Escard%C3%B3"} Martín Escardó
                (
                %a{:href => "Escardo09"}> Escardó 09
                ), who credits
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leopoldo+Nachbin"} Leopoldo Nachbin
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Z
                to be closed if the direct image mapping or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f: P(X) \to P(Z)
                preserves closed subsets, he uses instead the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f: P(X) \to P(Z)
                and asks that this preserve
                = succeed "." do
                  %em open sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
                in proving Proposition
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#onlyif"}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"} directed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                of the given space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo =
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = \mathcal{O}(X)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                by taking its inhabited open sets to be upward-closed subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subseteq \mathcal{O}(X)
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \cap \Sigma
                is inhabited. Using the open-set formulation of closed maps, he then argues that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                belongs to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/toddtrimble/published/Characterizations+of+compactness#escardo"} here
          %h2#consequences Consequences
          %h3#TychonoffTheorem Tychonoff theorem
          %p
            It has been observed by
            %a{:href => "#CT1996"} Clementino and Tholen
            that the closed-projection formulation of compactness (theorem
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#char"}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem"} Tychonoff theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
            of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact topological space
            is itself compact. First a very routine lemma.
          #LemmaTychonoff.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_i)_{i: I}
              be a family of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and subset
